.TH EGRAPH 1 2026-09-29 "egraph @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
egraph \- query the dependency graph of the installed packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B egraph
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command " [" command-options ] " [" arguments ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B egraph
keeps the dependency graph of a Gentoo system in a store file and answers questions about it in
milliseconds: what depends on a package, why it is installed, who links a soname, what is broken,
what
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove and what
.B emerge \-u
would merge.
.PP
The store is built by
.BR egraph-build (1),
which evaluates the installed packages through portage.
Every command first checks that the store still describes the system and brings it up to date
when it does not, unless
.B \-\-no\-refresh
says otherwise.
The answers are held to portage's own on generated systems and on a live one.
.PP
Without a command,
.B egraph
opens its interactive interface on a terminal (the same as
.BR "egraph tui" ),
and reads commands one per line otherwise (the same as
.BR "egraph shell" ).
.PP
Packages are named by installed cpv
.RI ( dev-libs/openssl-3.5.0 )
or cp
.RI ( dev-libs/openssl ,
every installed version); atoms take portage's syntax.
.PP
Run as
.BI egraph\- command
(a link to
.BR egraph ),
it runs that command:
.B egraph\-orphans
is
.BR "egraph orphans" ,
the global options taken among its own.
.SH OPTIONS
These come before the command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options, or a command's, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
The root whose installed packages to query (default
.BR $ROOT ,
else
.IR / ).
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-root " dir"
The root of the portage configuration to evaluate them with (default
.BR $PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT ).
.TP
.BI \-\-eprefix " dir"
The offset prefix of a prefix installation (default
.BR $PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX ).
.TP
.BI \-\-store " file"
The store to read (default
.BR $EGRAPH_STORE ,
else the system store; see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BI \-\-builder " command"
The
.B egraph-build
that refreshes the store (default
.BR $EGRAPH_BUILD ,
else the one next to
.BR egraph ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.BI \-\-emerge " command"
The
.B emerge
that
.B \-\-verify
and the actions run (default
.BR $EGRAPH_EMERGE ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.BI \-\-dispatch\-conf " command"
The
.B dispatch\-conf
an action offers when configuration updates are waiting (default
.BR $EGRAPH_DISPATCH_CONF ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
It takes the roots from
.BR ROOT ", " PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT " and " PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX .
.TP
.BI \-\-emaint " command"
The
.B emaint
that
.B sync
runs (default
.BR $EGRAPH_EMAINT ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ),
with the roots in the environment as for
.BR dispatch\-conf .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-refresh
Answer from a stale store instead of bringing it up to date.
.TP
.BR \-\-layout " auto|human|lines"
Results for people, or as tab-separated lines for scripts (default
.BR $EGRAPH_LAYOUT ,
else
.BR auto :
for people on a terminal).
.TP
.BR \-\-color " auto|always|never"
Colour the layout for people (default
.BR auto :
on a terminal, unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set).
.TP
.BR \-\-glyphs " nerd|unicode|ascii"
Icons in the layout for people: a Nerd Font's, plain Unicode, or ASCII (default
.BR $EGRAPH_GLYPHS ,
else
.B nerd
in a UTF-8 locale and
.B ascii
otherwise).
.SH COMMANDS
Commands that read dependencies take
.BR \-\-dynamic\-deps " y|n" :
read an installed package's dependencies from its ebuild when the same version is still in its
repository, as emerge's option of the name does (default
.BR y ).
Those that follow what keeps a package take
.BR \-\-with\-bdeps " y|n" :
whether build-time dependencies keep packages, as emerge's option does (default
.BR y ).
.SS deps \fIpackage\fR...
What installed packages depend on, with the atom and the kinds of dependency for each.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-possible
Also what the ebuilds would add with USE flags toggled, with the flags.
.SS rdeps \fIpackage\fR...
What depends on installed packages.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-possible
Also what the ebuilds would depend on them with USE flags toggled, with the flags.
.SS why \fIatom\fR
The shortest chain of dependencies from a root set
.RI ( @selected ", " @system ", " @profile )
that keeps each installed package the atom matches, as depclean finds it.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-with\-bdeps
.SS match \fIatom\fR...
The installed packages each atom matches.
.TP
.B \-\-candidates
Match the installed cps' ebuilds instead
.RI ( cpv::repo ),
with the USE each would be built with now, masked ones included.
.SS soname \fIsoname\fR
The installed packages that need a soname
.RI ( libssl.so.3 ).
.TP
.B \-\-providers
The packages providing it instead.
.SS broken
Installed dependencies that nothing installed satisfies.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.SS blockers \fR[\fIpackage\fR ...]
Blockers
.RB ( ! atom
and
.BR !! atom)
in the installed packages' dependencies, each with the installed packages it blocks: of the
packages given, every blocker they hold and every blocker holding them back; without packages,
the blockers that block something installed.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.SS orphans
The installed packages
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-with\-bdeps
.SS updates
The installed packages
.B emerge \-u
would replace or rebuild, and the new packages it would pull in, each with the USE it would be
built with as
.B emerge \-v
shows it; a new package beside installed ones in other slots of its name is a new slot
.RB ( new\-slot ,
emerge's NS), with those installed versions. Then the installed packages it would uninstall for a blocker between them and a merge,
and the blockers it could not resolve, for which it refuses the whole plan (exit status 6). On
the running system's root, emerge only resolves a strong blocker
.RB ( !! atom)
by a replacement.
A merge whose dependency nothing satisfies, or an ebuild with invalid metadata, is passed over for
another version, as emerge backtracks; when what emerge must merge has none left, the plan ends
with the dependencies no visible version matches, and emerge refuses it too.
So it does a version emerge would select whose REQUIRED_USE its USE leaves unsatisfied, even one
it then passes over: the plan ends with the constraints left unmet, which a
.I package.use
change must meet. A USE dependency no version meets as it would be built takes the USE change
emerge's autounmask asks for, on the best version that can take it: the plan is made with it
and ends with the
.I package.use
lines, each with what requires it, and emerge refuses it until they are written. Run from a
terminal, with the human layout,
.BR plan " and " updates
offer to write them: on yes they are appended to
.I package.use
under the configuration root (to
.I package.use/zz-autounmask
when that is a directory), and the plan is made again on stores refreshed for them.
.B \-\-verify
compares the USE changes emerge asks for, and only them: its merge list then is made before
some of them.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-held
Also the updates that installed dependents hold back, which of their atoms do, and the commands
that would get past them.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
In merge order, each with the places of the merges it waits for, each followed by its kinds:
.B b
build (DEPEND, BDEPEND),
.B i
install (IDEPEND),
.B r
run (RDEPEND),
.B p
post (PDEPEND),
.B l
emerge's implicit wait on a libc it merges, and
.B t
through installed packages it depends on.
A place after its own is a wait the order breaks.
An uninstall lists the places of the merges whose blockers need it gone: as emerge does, it
goes once they are merged, both installed meanwhile.
The human layout lists only the earlier places waited for by
.BR b ,
.B i
or
.BR r .
.TP
.B \-\-tree
Each merge under the root set and the packages it comes from.
.TP
.B \-\-world
Only the packages the root sets keep, as
.B emerge \-u @world
does.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
Every package in scope, not only the arguments and what their merges need, as
.B emerge \-\-deep
does; with
.BR \-\-world ,
what the versions the root sets move to depend on, through the one alternative of each
.B ||
emerge would take.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
Also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-newuse
makes for changed USE or IUSE.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
Also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-changed\-use
makes for changed USE.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Also run
.B emerge \-\-pretend
on the same request, under the same roots and without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
and show where its merge list differs: a package only one of them merges or uninstalls, a
different kind of merge, a new package's USE, or a blocker only one of them cannot resolve. The
differences go to standard error in the lines layout, and make the exit status 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-resume\-list " file"
Also write the updates to
.I file
as the merge list
.B emerge \-\-resume
reads (the
.I resume
entry of portage's
.IR mtimedb ,
as JSON), with
.B \-\-oneshot
among its options. emerge resolves the blockers and orders the merges again when it resumes;
the list carries no uninstalls. A plan emerge would refuse is not written, nor a request for
nothing (an empty set).
.TP
.BI \-\-requests " file"
Also write the updates to
.I file
as the requests
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-worker" " (" egraph\-build (1))
takes, a JSON object a line, in the order one worker would carry them out: each merge with the
installed packages it blocks or that block it then, and each uninstall straight after the merges
it waits for, as
.B emerge
runs them. Nothing joins the world file, as with
.BR \-\-oneshot .
Written as
.B \-\-resume\-list
is.
.SS plan \fItarget\fR...
What
.B emerge \-\-pretend
would merge for the targets, in the lines
.B updates
prints: atoms (a name without its category takes the one the repositories know it in) and the sets
.BR @world ", " @selected ", " @system ", " @profile ", " @installed
and
.BR @preserved\-rebuild ,
which
.B egraph\-build \-\-notices
loads as emerge does (as root: only root and the portage group may read the registry of
preserved libraries).
A package the request names is merged even when installed, as plain
.B emerge
does. A package only the repositories know is evaluated first by
.BR egraph\-build " \-\-evaluate"
(not with
.BR \-\-no\-refresh ),
and stays in the store until
.BR "egraph rebuild" .
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Update each installed slot a target matches instead, and add its best version's slot, as
.B emerge \-u
does.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
With
.BR \-u ,
every package the targets lead to through the versions they move to, and the one alternative
of each
.B ||
emerge would take, as
.B emerge \-\-deep
does.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-noreplace
Skip a target something installed matches, as
.B emerge \-\-noreplace
does.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
With
.BR \-u ,
also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-newuse
makes.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
With
.BR \-u ,
also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-changed\-use
makes.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
In merge order, each with the places of the merges it waits for, each followed by its kinds:
.B b
build (DEPEND, BDEPEND),
.B i
install (IDEPEND),
.B r
run (RDEPEND),
.B p
post (PDEPEND),
.B l
emerge's implicit wait on a libc it merges, and
.B t
through installed packages it depends on.
A place after its own is a wait the order breaks.
An uninstall lists the places of the merges whose blockers need it gone: as emerge does, it
goes once they are merged, both installed meanwhile.
The human layout lists only the earlier places waited for by
.BR b ,
.B i
or
.BR r .
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Also run
.B emerge \-\-pretend
on the same request, under the same roots and without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
and show where its merge list differs: a package only one of them merges, a different kind of
merge, or a new package's USE. The differences go to standard error in the lines layout, and
make the exit status 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-resume\-list " file"
As for
.BR updates ,
the targets among the list's favorites, which emerge selects once they are merged.
.TP
.BI \-\-requests " file"
As for
.BR updates ,
each merge an atom target matches recording the atom emerge would put in the world file, and
each uninstall one matches dropping what then matches nothing.
.SS update
Shows the updates as
.B updates
does, then has
.B emerge \-u \-\-oneshot
merge them, so that nothing it merges joins a set. Nothing is merged, and
.B emerge
is not run, when the plan is refused (exit status 6), when there is nothing to merge, or when the
installed packages' database under the root cannot be written (exit status 1: run
.B egraph
as root). Otherwise the same request goes to
.B emerge \-\-pretend
first, as
.B \-\-verify
sends it, and nothing is merged if its merge list differs (exit status 5). Then
.B egraph
asks, and on yes runs
.B emerge
on the request under the same roots: without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
but for its options that change only how emerge carries out a plan
.RB ( \-\-jobs ", " \-\-load\-average ", " \-\-keep\-going ", " \-\-quiet\-build ", " \-\-quiet\-fail ,
.BR \-\-fail\-clean ", " \-\-buildpkg ", " \-\-buildpkg\-exclude ", " \-\-alert ", " \-\-color ,
.BR \-\-quiet ", " \-\-nospinner ),
read through
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-emerge\-options" .
Messages the ebuilds logged follow, read from what elog's
.B save_summary
module appended to its log while emerge ran (emerge's own
.B echo
of them is left out then, when the log holds every class it shows). The stores are refreshed
as soon as emerge ends, even when it fails (exit status 1), and what
.B notices
lists follows; run from a terminal without
.BR \-\-yes ,
configuration updates waiting offer
.BR dispatch\-conf .
Run from a terminal, a plan that needs USE changes offers to write them first, as
.B updates
does, and is made again. Without a terminal to ask on, or in the shell, only
.B \-\-yes
merges (exit status 2 otherwise); the interface runs no actions yet.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-held
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
.TP
.B \-\-tree
.TP
.B \-\-world
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
As for
.BR updates .
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking, and offer no USE changes.
.SS install \fItarget\fR...
Shows the plan for the targets as
.B plan
does, then has
.B emerge
merge it, as
.B update
does. The targets join
.B @selected
as
.B emerge
adds them, unless
.BR \-\-oneshot .
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-noreplace
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
As for
.BR plan .
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-oneshot
Add the targets to no set, as
.B emerge \-\-oneshot
does.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking, and offer no USE changes.
.SS exec \fItarget\fR...
Shows the plan for the targets and verifies it as
.B install
does, then merges it without
.BR emerge :
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-worker" " (" egraph\-build (1))
processes build the packages, as many at once as the jobs allow, and merge them one at a time,
as
.B emerge
schedules them with
.B FEATURES=merge\-wait
(portage's default): a build starts once nothing it reaches through what each package waits for
is still to merge, a built package merges once no build runs, in the order the builds finished,
and each package a blocker needs gone is uninstalled once the merges that need it gone are done.
A line is shown for each phase as it starts. With more than one job, what the builds print goes
only to their logs, and a build starts beside others only while
.B PORTAGE_TMPDIR
has the free space
.B emerge \-\-jobs\-tmpdir\-require\-free\-gb
asks (18 GiB by default, and 1 GiB for each build running). Under
.B FEATURES=jobserver\-token
each build takes a token from the jobserver
.B MAKEFLAGS
names, as
.B emerge
does. A package that fails is named with the phase and its build log; nothing new starts after
it, but the builds running finish and what built still merges. The targets join
.B @selected
as
.B emerge
adds them, unless
.BR \-\-oneshot .
Also run as
.BR egraph\-exec .
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-noreplace
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
As for
.BR plan .
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-oneshot
Add the targets to no set, as
.B emerge \-\-oneshot
does.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Merge without asking, and offer no USE changes.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs " \fIN\fR"
Build up to
.I N
packages at once, as
.B emerge \-\-jobs
does; otherwise as many as
.B EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS
says, one by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-trace " file"
Write to
.I file
a line as each build, merge and uninstall starts and ends: the seconds since the run started,
what happened
.RB ( build\-start ", " built ", " build\-failed ", " merge\-start ", " merged ", "
.BR merge\-failed ", " uninstall\-start ", " uninstalled " or " uninstall\-failed )
and the cpv, separated by tabs.
.SS remove \fIpackage\fR...
What
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove of the installed packages the atoms (or exact cpvs) match: each one nothing else
keeps, as depclean weighs arguments, keeping every installed package they do not match and
leaving
.B @selected
out, since it deselects them. A package that stays is listed with the packages that need it and
the sets that keep it. Then, as
.B update
does, the request goes to
.B emerge \-\-pretend \-\-depclean
first, nothing is removed if it would remove otherwise (exit status 5), and on yes
.B emerge \-\-depclean
removes them and the stores are refreshed.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-\-with\-bdeps " y|n"
Whether build-time dependencies keep packages, as emerge's option (default
.BR y ).
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking.
.SS select \fIpackage\fR...
Adds the atoms and sets to
.BR @selected ,
as
.B emerge \-\-select \-\-noreplace
does: shows the plan for what is not installed yet as
.B plan \-n
does, then, as
.B update
does, verifies it, asks, and runs
.BR emerge .
emerge decides the atom it records; once the stores are refreshed, the atoms that joined
.B @selected
are listed. After any action, so are the atoms that joined or left it.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking.
.SS deselect \fIpackage\fR...
What
.B emerge \-\-deselect
would remove from the world file, and from
.I world_sets
for a set, as
.B emerge \-\-pretend \-\-deselect
says, followed by the installed packages
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would then remove that it would not remove now. On yes, as
.B update
does,
.B emerge \-\-deselect
removes them; nothing is merged or removed.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking.
.SS sync
.B emaint sync \-\-auto
syncs the repositories set to auto-sync, as
.B emerge \-\-sync
does, and the stores are refreshed; then the updates, as
.B updates
shows them with the same options, and the notices, the news the sync brought among them.
Whether the repositories can be written is for
.B emaint
to judge: syncing as a user who cannot fails there. The updates and notices follow a failed
sync too, for the repositories that synced; the exit status is then 1.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-held
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
.TP
.B \-\-tree
.TP
.B \-\-world
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
.TP
.B \-\-verify
As for
.BR updates .
.SS notices
What needs attention once
.B emerge
has run, as it reports it: the configuration files under
.B CONFIG_PROTECT
with
.I ._cfg
updates waiting (which portage finds only where the user can write), the repositories'
unread news items with their titles, and the libraries emerge preserved for what still uses
them, with the packages that do, read through
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-notices" .
As lines, an update is its file,
.BR config
and the update; a news item is the item,
.BR news ,
its repository and its title; a preserved library is its path,
.BR preserved ,
the package it comes from and the packages using it, space-separated; and each atom of
.B @preserved\-rebuild
follows with
.BR rebuild .
After an action run from a terminal without
.BR \-\-yes ,
preserved libraries have
.B egraph install \-\-oneshot @preserved\-rebuild
planned and offered at once, not again after that rebuild.
.SS export \fR[\fIpackage\fR...]
Part of the graph, or all of it without packages, for other tools.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " dot|json"
Graphviz or JSON (default
.BR dot ).
.TP
.B \-\-evaluated
The evaluated store instead, whole and as JSON: the dependencies emerge reads by default, and the
versions each installed package could move to.
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " n"
Dependency edges to follow out from the packages (default 1).
.TP
.BR \-\-direction " reverse|forward|both"
Which edges to follow (default
.BR reverse ).
.SS stats
Store and graph statistics.
.SS tui
The interactive interface: the installed packages, pending updates, orphans and broken ones, each
package's page, the plan, the running emerges, and a
.B :
prompt taking these commands. It keeps the store current while it is open.
.SS shell
Commands read one per line from standard input, the stores loaded once; the global options that
choose the stores are taken from the command line only.
.SS rebuild
Rebuild the store from scratch.
.SS refresh
Bring the store up to date if what it was built from changed, printing nothing.
.SS check
Compare the store with a fresh build, exiting 4 when they differ.
.SS affected
What a set of merges can affect, as JSON, for portage's neighborhood completion.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BI \-\-request " file"
The JSON request (default
.BR \- :
standard input).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR ROOT ", " PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT ", " PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX
Defaults for
.BR \-\-root ", " \-\-config\-root " and " \-\-eprefix .
.TP
.BR EGRAPH_STORE ", " EGRAPH_BUILD ", " EGRAPH_EMERGE ", " EGRAPH_DISPATCH_CONF ", " EGRAPH_EMAINT ,
.BR EGRAPH_LAYOUT ", " EGRAPH_GLYPHS
Defaults for
.BR \-\-store ", " \-\-builder ", " \-\-emerge ", " \-\-dispatch\-conf ", " \-\-emaint ,
.BR \-\-layout " and " \-\-glyphs .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, no colour unless
.B \-\-color always
asks for it.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where the user's own store goes (default
.IR ~/.cache ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/cache/egraph/installed.egraph
The system store, with the evaluated store beside it; written by root.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME}/egraph/installed.egraph
The store of a user who cannot write the system store, used while the system store is not
current.
.TP
.I /etc/portage/postsync.d/egraph
Refreshes the store after a sync.
.TP
.I /etc/portage/post_emerge.d/egraph
Refreshes the store after an emerge that changed the installed packages, once the dispatcher is
linked in place.
.TP
.I /usr/share/egraph/post_emerge
The dispatcher that runs every hook in
.IR /etc/portage/post_emerge.d .
portage runs only
.I /etc/portage/bin/post_emerge
after an emerge, which is the user's own file; without one, link this there.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as a package that is not installed or a store that could not be built.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed.
